# SquadRidge — components

Build components for a high-trust, privacy-first, conflict-resolution platform. Components must feel calm, modern, durable, and operationally clear under pressure.

## Non-negotiable principles

1. **Accessibility first**
   - Meet WCAG AA at minimum.
   - Normal text must meet at least 4.5:1 contrast.
   - Large text must meet at least 3:1 contrast.
   - UI components, icons, focus indicators, chart marks, and input boundaries must meet at least 3:1 contrast against adjacent colors.
   - Never rely on color alone to convey state, warning, success, trend, or urgency; pair color with text, icon, pattern, or shape.

2. **Calm clarity**
   - Components must communicate clearly without alarmist visual language.
   - Use restrained emphasis, not aggressive motion or high-noise styling.
   - Warnings and tension signals should support de-escalation and comprehension.

3. **Performance-aware polish**
   - Keep bundles small.
   - Avoid heavy decorative animation, especially on low-bandwidth or high-stress paths.
   - Motion must be purposeful, subtle, and removable under reduced-motion preferences.

4. **Consistency over novelty**
   - Use shared tokens, variants, spacing, and states.
   - Avoid one-off component styling unless it is justified and documented.
   - Prefer reusable variants over ad hoc class strings.

## Theme and visual system

- Default product shell uses deep indigo/navy surfaces from `--sr-bg` and `--sr-bg-elevated` in `tokens.css` — never hardcode hex in components.
- Primary actions use `--sr-primary` (teal); all interactive states must be tokenized and contrast-checked.
- Warning colors use `--sr-warning` only with sufficient contrast and paired text/icon support.
- Dark surfaces must preserve readability for dense operational views, not just look visually dramatic.

## Typography

- Use tokenized fonts from `src/styles/tokens.css`: Inter for body/UI, IBM Plex Sans for headings, IBM Plex Serif for marketing display, IBM Plex Mono for ledger/code.
- Do not hardcode `font-family` in components — use Tailwind `font-sans`, `font-display`, `font-heading`, or `font-mono`.
- Use fluid type with `clamp()` only where scaling materially improves layout, such as page headings or responsive section headers (`--sr-text-display`, `--sr-text-h1`, etc.).
- Do not use oversized marketing typography in product UI (`/app/*` chrome uses the compact app scale).
- Maintain a clear type hierarchy for:
  - page titles
  - section headings
  - body
  - labels
  - metadata
  - button text

## Spacing and layout

- Use the Tailwind spacing scale consistently.
- Maintain visual rhythm across cards, tables, forms, drawers, and modals.
- Dense operational views are acceptable, but never at the cost of readability or tap-target size.
- Interactive elements must remain usable on mobile and pointer devices.
- Large tap targets are required for intervention actions such as “Slow down” and “Pull back.”

## Component state requirements

Every interactive component must define:
- default state
- hover state
- focus-visible state
- active/pressed state
- disabled state
- loading state where relevant
- error state where relevant
- empty state where relevant

Do not ship components with missing or ambiguous states.

## Buttons

- Primary buttons use the designated action color token.
- Buttons must have clear hover, pressed, focus, and disabled states.
- Button labels must be action-led and explicit.
- Do not rely on color fill alone; destructive, warning, or secondary actions should also differ through label, icon, border, or hierarchy.
- Maintain adequate target size for touch and keyboard users.

## Forms

- All inputs must have visible labels.
- Do not rely on placeholder text as the only label.
- Required, invalid, success, and helper states must use text and icons in addition to color.
- Error messages must be specific and adjacent to the relevant field.
- Inputs on dark backgrounds must preserve strong border and text contrast.

## Tables and dense tools

- Moderation tools, admin tools, and operational dashboards may use dense tables/cards, but must remain readable on dark surfaces.
- Use tabular numerals where metrics or time values matter.
- Support keyboard navigation and clear focus movement for dense tools.
- Sticky headers, overflow handling, and mobile fallback behavior must be intentional.

## Sentiment / tension UI

- Show trend using color plus label/icon, never color alone.
- Prefer calm wording and directional guidance over deterministic emotional judgments.
- Surface uncertainty when confidence is low.
- Do not turn sentiment visuals into punitive or sensational UI.

## Interventions

- Intervention controls such as “Slow down” and “Pull back” must be prominent, calm, and easy to tap.
- Intervention components should visually de-escalate the interface, not intensify it.
- Use supportive copy, generous spacing, and stable layout behavior.

## SVG and iconography

- Keep SVG style simple and consistent in stroke, fill, corner treatment, and visual weight.
- Avoid violent, militarized, nationalistic, or inflammatory imagery, including flags and weapon motifs.
- Icons must support comprehension, not replace labels in critical workflows.
- Decorative SVG should not introduce ambiguity or visual clutter.

## Motion

- Motion must be subtle and purposeful.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- Avoid decorative looping animation in critical communication, moderation, or safety flows.
- Use motion for feedback, hierarchy, and orientation, not spectacle.

## Code standards

- Use React function components and hooks.
- Use explicit TypeScript props and exported prop types where appropriate.
- Prefer variant props and composable APIs over one-off style overrides.
- Use Tailwind for styling, but avoid class sprawl that hides component intent.
- Keep business logic out of purely presentational components when possible.
- Accessibility props, keyboard handling, and semantic structure are part of the component contract.

## Testing requirements

- Verify contrast for all text, controls, icons, and data visual marks.
- Verify keyboard access and visible focus states.
- Verify components in dark theme and any light/admin/demo variants.
- Verify reduced-motion behavior.
- Verify mobile readability and target sizing.
- Verify warning, trend, and status components still make sense without color perception.